Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 04:40:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 52/52] feat(mcp): read part of a frame, and outline it,
 instead of the whole HTML

get_frame returned a frame's whole HTML, the only text view MCP agents
had. On a real canvas of 271 frames that is ~3.4k tokens at the median,
~5.9k at p90 and ~13k at p99, mostly inlined CSS, font and token blocks
an edit does not touch. The resident agent already had two cheaper
views, inspect_frame and a bounded get_frame_html; MCP agents had
neither. Now they have both:

- get_frame keeps returning the whole HTML by default, and on request
  reads only: element_id (the source of the element carrying that
  data-doop-id, children included), query (up to five snippets around a
  literal text) or offset/limit (1,000-30,000 chars).
- inspect_frame, under the resident's name: the rendered outline, up to
  64 semantic elements with selector (a data-doop-id when there is one),
  text, box and computed colour and font, plus the frame's palette,
  type and CSS variables. A few thousand tokens whatever the HTML's
  size, and its boxes and ids feed get_frame element_id and
  get_frame_screenshot's crops.

The query and offset reads move out of resident.ts into
server/frameHtmlRead.ts, so both agents run one implementation; the
resident's text is unchanged. The agent instructions say to outline a
large frame first and read and check only the part being edited.

Prior art: Playwright MCP sends an accessibility snapshot rather than
the page source, and Figma's Dev Mode MCP gives a sparse metadata
outline for large selections before any code.

Tests: mcpFrameRead.test.ts (each read, its errors, and the outline in
real Chromium).
